<title>GERMANY: Mercedes, workers agree to cost cuts.</title>
<headline>Mercedes, workers agree to cost cuts.</headline>
<dateline>MANNHEIM, Germany 1997-02-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>German car maker Mercedes-Benz AG said on Friday workers at a bus plant agreed to a plan expected to cut costs by about 100 million marks ($60.3 million) annually through the year 2000.</p>
<p>The workers agreed to accept a two percent cut in sick time, reduced break time, and a flexible work week that could fall to 30 hours in low-demand cycles, Mercedes said.</p>
<p>Mercedes would reduce the plants payroll through attrition and would grant interested workers leaves of absence of six months to three years.</p>
<p>In exchange the company agreed to keep open the plant, which produced 3,100 buses and 178,000 diesel motors in 1996.</p>
<p>-- Bonn Newsroom, +49-228 26097150 ($ = 1.659 German Marks)</p>
